{"id": "99aa105d-a16a-4b10-a450-d61f55ebad82", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "ECR lifecycle policies plus checking what's actually running (ECS tasks/services and EKS pods) means pulling repo lists, policy rules, image manifests, and live workload data — that's a lot of raw data I'd rather not drag into this chat. Let me look into it.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_ovCV4EcaXUCbhz12PZHeys", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "For every ECR repository in account 012345678901_1, region us-east-1, determine whether the repository's lifecycle policy would (now or on its next evaluation) delete an image tag/digest that is currently running in production workloads. Summarize findings per repository.",
  "context": "User wants a safety audit: are any ECR lifecycle expiration/cleanup rules at risk of deleting images that are actively deployed and running right now? Need to cross-reference ECR lifecycle policy rules (tagStatus, countType, countNumber/sinceImagePushed, tagPrefixList, etc.) against the actual image tags/digests currently in use.",
  "resources": [
    "All ECR repositories in account 012345678901_1, region us-east-1",
    "All ECS clusters/services/task definitions in account 012345678901_1, region us-east-1 (to find images currently running in ECS tasks)",
    "All EKS clusters in account 012345678901_1, region us-east-1 (use kubectl get pods -A to find images currently running in pods, including image digests if possible)"
  ],
  "timeframe": "current state as of now",
  "criteria": "Steps: 1) List all ECR repos in the region. 2) For each repo, get its lifecycle policy (get_lifecycle_policy / list rules). 3) For each repo, list images (describe_images) to know tags, push dates, and digests. 4) Discover running workloads: list ECS clusters -> services -> task definitions -> container image references (repo:tag or digest); list EKS clusters -> use kubectl to get all pod images across namespaces. 5) For each running image reference, resolve to its ECR repo + tag/digest + pushed date. 6) Evaluate each repo's lifecycle policy rules against that image to determine if the rule would currently select/expire it for deletion (e.g., image falls outside the 'keep last N tagged/untagged images' window, or is older than 'sinceImagePushed' days, or matches a tagPrefixList expiry rule). 7) Produce a per-repository summary: repo name, lifecycle policy summary, whether any running image is at risk of deletion by that policy, and which specific image tag/digest is at risk and why. If a repo has no lifecycle policy, note that explicitly (no risk). If no ECS/EKS workloads reference a given repo's images at all, note that explicitly as 'not currently running anywhere found'. Also note any images you could not confidently match to a running workload due to limited visibility.",
  "aws_account_id": "012345678901_1",
  "aws_region": "us-east-1"
}", "deep_research": false}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit ECR lifecycle policies against running ECS/EKS images in us-east-1"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "a37b1dd0-0bbb-4a1d-8687-7a7b3bf95d8c", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "No explicit deep research marker is present, so this defaults to shallow research, but the task's thorough cross-referencing request pushes me toward a reasonably comprehensive approach anyway. I'll focus on gathering core data: ECR repos, lifecycle policies, images, plus ECS clusters, services, and task definitions.

I don't have kubectl access or any boto3 equivalent for listing pods, since that's a Kubernetes API concern rather than an AWS API one, so EKS pod-level image data is out of reach -- I'll just note the clusters exist and flag this limitation. Time to start pulling the ECR and ECS data.

"}], "parent_id": "254916ca-58e5-4b51-bfba-4b01049c0ce0"}

{"id": "ce559b15-fbe4-41b9-a39e-8caf54300077", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "No ECS clusters at all, which simplifies things since there's no ECS workloads to worry about. But I should also check Lambda functions using container images, since those might reference ECR images as running workloads too.

Since the task really only cares about ECS and EKS, and both are empty, there's nothing currently running that would be affected by changes to these repos - still need to gather lifecycle policies and images for the audit itself.

"}], "parent_id": "254916ca-58e5-4b51-bfba-4b01049c0ce0"}
